---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { LABELS, commons } from '../config/commons';

const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Standards' }];

const REQUIRED = [
	{
		field: 'label',
		why: 'What kind of evidence this is. A public record and a constructed illustration are both useful and are not the same thing, and a reader has to know which one they are holding before they read a word of it.',
	},
	{
		field: 'labelNote',
		why: 'Why it carries that label. A label with no reasoning behind it is an assertion, and assertions are what this site exists to avoid.',
	},
	{
		field: 'provenance',
		why: 'Where the account came from and what was checked before it published. This is the field a researcher reads first and the one that decides whether the rest is usable.',
	},
	{
		field: 'whatHappened',
		why: 'The facts, dated and placed. An undated account of an unnamed state is not evidence of anything, because insurance rules differ by both.',
	},
	{
		field: 'decidedBy',
		why: 'Who decided, or that nobody did. This is the field the whole property turns on. It records the decision-maker; it never records whether they were right.',
	},
	{
		field: 'cannotGeneralize',
		why: 'At least three reasons this does not transfer. The likeliest harm from a site like this is a reader treating somebody else’s outcome as their own rule, and this is the field that argues against that on every page.',
	},
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="What makes an account worth citing"
	eyebrow="Standards"
	lede="Six required fields, a label on every report, and one prohibition that survives from the Record. This is why a moderated account here is usable evidence and a forum post is not."
	crumbs={crumbs}
>
	<section class="band band-tight">
		<div class="shell page-grid">
			<div class="prose">
				<h2>The problem this solves</h2>
				<p>
					An anonymous thread is not citable. "My claim got denied and it was unfair" carries no
					date, no jurisdiction, no line of business, no document, and no way for anyone to tell
					whether it happened at all. A thousand of those do not add up to authority; they add up
					to noise that a careful reader learns to discount, and that is what most insurance
					discussion online already is.
				</p>
				<p>
					What makes lived experience usable is structure and provenance. Not more words — more
					fields.
				</p>

				<h2>The six required fields</h2>
				<dl class="fields">
					{
						REQUIRED.map((r) => (
							<div>
								<dt>{r.field}</dt>
								<dd>{r.why}</dd>
							</div>
						))
					}
				</dl>
				<p>
					These are not this site's invention. They are the schema the Record already uses for its
					own worked examples, which is the reason the Commons could be specified in a day: the
					structure that makes an account citable was already built and already in use. What was
					missing was a moderated way in.
				</p>

				<h2>The labels, and what each one commits us to</h2>
				<dl class="fields">
					{
						Object.entries(LABELS).map(([key, meaning]) => (
							<div>
								<dt>{key}</dt>
								<dd>{meaning}</dd>
							</div>
						))
					}
				</dl>
				<p>
					A composite or a hypothetical may never be presented as something that happened to a
					particular person. That rule is carried over from the Record unchanged, and it is
					checked at moderation rather than trusted.
				</p>

				<h2>The prohibition</h2>
				<div class="callout" data-tone="stop">
					<h3>No verdicts, from anyone</h3>
					<p>{commons.verdictProhibition}</p>
				</div>
				<p>
					The honest form of the thing a verdict is reaching for is already a field. Every account
					records <code>decidedBy</code>: the adjuster, the department, the court, the appraisal
					panel — or, where the situation was never adjudicated at all, the sentence the Record's
					own examples use:
				</p>
				<blockquote>
					<p>No authority decided this. It is illustrative only.</p>
				</blockquote>
				<p>
					That sentence is the model for this entire site. Recording who decided is evidence.
					Declaring who was right is a coverage determination, and only an insurer or its
					authorised representative can make one.
				</p>

				<h2>How this relates to the Record</h2>
				<p>
					Reports cite <a href={commons.record.origin} rel="noopener">{commons.record.name}</a>{' '}
					where they touch law, by claim address, so a reader can follow an account straight to the
					document behind the rule it turned on.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>The Record does not cite back.</strong> That is deliberate and permanent. Its
					citable unit is a sentence backed by a published document, and mixing attributed human
					accounts into it would cost the property the only asset it has. Layers point downward
					here: this side may cite that one, and never the reverse.
				</p>
			</div>

			<aside aria-label="Page contents">
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>On this page</h2>
					<ol>
						<li><a href="#">The problem</a></li>
						<li><a href="/moderation">How moderation works</a></li>
						<li><a href="/contribute">What contributing involves</a></li>
					</ol>
				</div>
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>Required fields</h2>
					<ul>{REQUIRED.map((r) => <li>{r.field}</li>)}</ul>
				</div>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
